      "status": "independently reviewed",
      "correspondence": "The finite-order rational transformation is killed by the prime-to-p and prime-to-q reductions on all its powers. The residual multiplier fixes a nonzero T-torsion value, forcing congruence to 1. The existence of the tied forms, local diamonds and the local torsion-reduction theorem are referenced inputs.",
      "review": "A second reader checked how the rational element's power enters the trivial stabilizer, how the two auxiliary primes eliminate its torsion, and how the nonzero level functional forces each multiplier to equal one."
